### What I Looked At

I looked over a classmate - Issa Ali's RegisterScreen implementation> I looked at how his registration form and how it handled user input validation and navigation. I also looked over the updates that was made to the string resources and reveiwed how they connected to the UI in the registration screen.

### What I Noticed

The one thing that I noticed was  the registration screen had included validation through the attemptRegister() function. Instead of allowing registration immediately it waits and the function checks whether the display name, email, username, password, and confirm password fields all have the values and whether the passwords match before continuing. This caught my eye because my implementation only handled navigation whenever I touched the sign Up button. Issas implementation adds the additional layer of user feedback that prevents users from moving forward with invalid data.

I also noticed that most of the labels, prompts, and button text were put into the strings.xml rather than being hardcoded into the composable. I think this improves maintainability because the UI text can be updated from one location instead of it uopdating throughout the codebase. It  also would make it a lot easier to support multiple languages in the future.

### Comments I Left

I commented the validation logic was a really smart/strong improvement because it helps guide any of the users through the registration process and it also doesn't allow for any invalid submissions. I suggested that because a RegisterViewModel is already there and present, any future improvements could move validation logic into the ViewModel  that way the business logic is separated from the UI layer which makes it easier to test and maintain.

