## One Thing I Understood More Deeply

When I was building the registration screen I feel like I gained a better understanding of how state drives the user interface in Jetpack Compose. Before this week I kinda knew that remember and mutableStateOf were needed for the text fields but I saw them as required syntax. After adding in the Display Name, Username, Email, Password, and Confirm Password fields. I saw how compose automatically updated the UI if any one of those state values changes. The text fields remain synchronized together with user input since the UI reacts to state changes instead of needing manual refreshes.

Another thing that I started to understand more was the purpose of callback functions like  onRegisterSuccess() and onNavigateToLogin(). At first I just thought the navigation would happen inside the screen itself but I learned that the Composable only signals that an had occurred while another part of the application handles navigation. Understanding that separation helped me really see how Compose keeps UI code more focused on presentation. This I feel like allowed application behavior to be managed elsewhere.

